Choose a point $P$. The normal-coordinate theorem says that one can construct coordinates centered at $P$ for which $\begin{aligned}
 g_{mn}(P)&=\delta_{mn}, \\
 \partial_r g_{mn}(P)&=0. \end{aligned}$ The lecture calls these Gaussian normal coordinates. Geometrically, one draws axes through $P$ and extends their coordinate lines as straight as the geometry permits. The precise notion of straightness will become geodesic motion.

